        <div class="hero-grid-main">
          <div class="hero-copy">
            <h1>How to guide child in career selection India: a process, not a pitch</h1>
            <p class="line strong">
              How to guide a child in career selection in India comes down to five moves, done in order. Start the conversation early. Ask questions instead of handing over conclusions. Use a free assessment as one input, not a verdict. Check real market demand together. Then agree on one small proof step before any big money or years get committed. Indian research backs this order up directly: parental encouragement raises a child's confidence in their own career decisions, while parental pressure without real back-and-forth is linked to higher anxiety and weaker follow-through. The real goal is not picking one "correct" job title. It is helping your child build a real skill portfolio and visible proof of work — the combination that opens income opportunities and helps them stand on their own feet earlier, whatever field they choose.
            </p>

            <div class="key-takeaways">
              <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
              <ul>
                <li>Guiding is a process across months, not a single sit-down lecture — start informal conversations by class 8-9, well before the class 10 stream decision forces the issue.</li>
                <li>Ask "what excites you about this?" instead of announcing "you should do this" — Indian studies on parental involvement link open, autonomy-supporting conversations to stronger career decision confidence in the child.</li>
                <li>Use a free psychometric or aptitude assessment to shrink a confusing list of options, never as the final word — self-reported mood, coaching, and question wording can all shift a test result.</li>
                <li>About two-thirds of Indian high-schoolers report real academic pressure from parents; the fix researchers point to is not lower ambition, it is replacing repeated opinions with a shared scorecard both of you fill in together.</li>
                <li>NEP 2020's multidisciplinary structure and CUET's subject-neutral entry mean a stream choice at 15 is far more reversible today than it was for your own generation — this should lower the stakes of the conversation, not raise them.</li>
                <li><strong>Your real job is to run the process alongside your child, not to hand them a pre-decided answer or hand them total freedom with zero structure.</strong></li>
              </ul>
            </div>

            <p class="line">Worried that the wrong conversation today turns into a wall of silence by next year?</p>
            <p class="line">Not sure if you should push your opinion, stay quiet, or something in between?</p>
            <p class="line">Tired of every relative and WhatsApp forward giving conflicting advice about what your child "should" do?</p>
            <p class="line">Then stop trying to win the argument.</p>
            <p class="line">Start running one structured process with your child, on paper, together.</p>

            <div class="cta-pair">
              <a class="btn-gold pulse" href={guidanceUrl}>Book a family career guidance session</a>
              <a class="btn-outline cta-assessment-lite" href={assessmentsUrl}>Free career and skill assessments</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <aside class="hero-board card">
            <div class="board-kicker">The 5-move process</div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>1</span>
              <p>Start early — informal talk from class 8-9, not a single big decision day.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>2</span>
              <p>Ask, don't announce — open questions beat handed-down conclusions.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="board-row">
              <span>3</span>
              <p>Test as input, not verdict — one input among several, never the final word.</p>
            </div>
